      "snippet": "[Letterboxd Review of \"The Ring\"] Hitchcock #50Just like contemporary films Shame and Alienated, great-grandaddy The Ring smoothly brings up the same item that symbolizes relationships through the good and the bad; the very symbol that endorses a promise and infinite sacrifice. It even does this while matter-of-factly referring to the boxing ring. What Carl Brisson's Sanders fights for is the literal metaphor for the lengths one will go for love. Something as temperamental as melodrama demands this kind of set-up; a triangle built upon jealousy, success, and to a farther extent power. Hitchcock pulls all the punches in his bag",
